# {{ project_name }}

A native Android app written in Python with
[Pydrud](https://github.com/MurShidM01/Pydrud) {{ pydrud_version }}.

The starter is **Heartbeat** — one calm screen with a softly pulsing heart, a
live BPM reading, a scrolling ECG trace and a compact transport button. The
motion is a Python frame loop, the look is a PSS stylesheet, and the palette
is generated from one brand colour. Open `src/app/screens/heartbeat.py` to
shape the screen, `src/app/theme.py` to re-brand it, or `src/app/theme.pss`
to tune its visual design.

## Run it

```bash
pydrud run              # build, install, launch with Flutter-style Hot Reload (r/R)
pydrud dev              # host Python + LAN live-preview QR (requires a Pydash client)
python run.py           # headless: build the widget tree on your computer
python -m pytest -q tests # run the project tests
```

`pydrud dev` and `pydrud run` are separate workflows: preview never builds an
APK, while `run` uses the generated Android app and embedded Python runtime.

## Project layout

```
{{ project_name }}/
├── run.py                  dev runner (headless / bridge client)
├── pydrud.yaml             Android SDK, NDK, package name and versions
├── pydrud.toml             Chaquopy Python packages and theme seed
├── assets/                 images and fonts shipped with the app
├── android/                generated native layer — `pydrud sync` refreshes it
├── tests/                  app tests driven by pydrud.testing.AppTester
└── src/
    ├── pydrud_config.py    Android runtime configuration
    └── app/
        ├── main.py         route registration + start-up entry point
        ├── config.py       app name, status lines and the BPM band
        ├── theme.py        the palette, generated from one brand colour
        ├── state.py        the State objects every screen shares
        ├── runtime.py      the router and the live App handle (`refresh()`)
        ├── jobs.py         background work (WorkManager)
        ├── theme.pss       starter stylesheet (classes via `class_=`)
        ├── ui.py           compatibility imports for older generated apps
        ├── components/     reusable UI shared by multiple screens
