```

## Android configuration

`pydrud.yaml` is the source of truth for the generated Android project:
app/package identity, release version, SDK/NDK/toolchain versions, ABIs,
assets, permissions, capabilities and deep links. Edit it and run
`pydrud sync`. Keep Python packages and the theme seed in `pydrud.toml`; app
code under `src/app/` is never overwritten by sync.

The starter needs no special Android permissions or capabilities.
Add only the features your app uses with Python-first commands such as
`pydrud capabilities add haptics` or `pydrud permissions add camera`; do not
hand-edit generated Android files.

CameraX and ML Kit (`CameraPreview`, `page.camera`, barcode/QR scanning) are
opt-in because they add roughly 10 MB of native libraries to the APK. They
are off until you set `camera: true` in `pydrud.yaml` — or just run
`pydrud permissions add camera` — and `pydrud sync`. Until then the widget
renders its fallback child and camera calls report how to enable them.

## PSS stylesheets

`src/app/theme.pss` is the starter stylesheet: its classes are attached in
the screens with `class_`, so editing the file re-skins the app without
touching Python. Put more `*.pss` files anywhere under `src/`; Pydrud
discovers them and reloads stylesheet changes without re-executing Python.
Selectors can target widget types, explicit keys, or Python-side classes
attached with `class_=`. Inline `style=` values override stylesheet
declarations. A syntax error keeps that file's last known-good rules active
and reports a diagnostic; moving or deleting a stylesheet removes its old
rules on the next rebuild. `pydrud analyze` validates stylesheets along with
your Python code. See the Pydrud
[PSS stylesheet guide](https://github.com/MurShidM01/Pydrud/blob/main/docs/PSS_STYLESHEETS.md)
for syntax and examples.

## Add a screen

1. Create `src/app/screens/profile.py` with a `profile_screen(page)` function.
2. Export it from `src/app/screens/__init__.py`.
3. Register it from `src/app/main.py`: `router.define("profile", profile_screen)`.
4. Navigate to it from a handler with `router.push("profile")`.

## Useful commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `pydrud run` | build, install and launch on an attached Android device |
| `pydrud dev` | run Python locally and wait for an authenticated Pydash preview client |
| `pydrud sync` | apply `pydrud.yaml` and regenerate managed Android files |
| `pydrud analyze` | static checks on your Python UI code and stylesheets |
| `pydrud pip add requests` | add a Python package to the APK build |
| `pydrud permissions add camera` | add a permission to the manifest |
| `pydrud capabilities add haptics` | enable generated feature bundles |
| `pydrud build --debug` | build a debuggable debug APK (the default variant) |
| `pydrud build --release` | build a release APK |
| `pydrud doctor` | check your Android toolchain |
